      "section_id": "abdominal_retractor_section_1",
      "section_title": "Abdominal retractor",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "abdominal_retractor_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An abdominal retractor is an instrument used to hold the abdominal wall, wound edges or internal organs away from the operative field to improve visibility and access during abdominal surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unlike a specifically named instrument such as the Balfour retractor, “abdominal retractor” is a broad category rather than one unique instrument design. It includes both handheld and self-retaining retractors."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is an abdominal surgical retractor. It is used to retract the abdominal wall or selected intra-abdominal tissues to improve exposure during abdominal operations. Its specific type determines whether it is handheld or self-retaining and whether it is intended for superficial, deep or organ retraction.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_2",
              "group_title": "Identification card",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Instrument category"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Main purpose"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maintain exposure of the abdominal operative field"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Types"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Handheld and self-retaining"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examples"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Deaver, Richardson, Morris, malleable ribbon and Balfour retractors"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "abdominal_retractor_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "2. Classification of abdominal retractors",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Abdominal retractors can be classified according to how they are held and the tissues they are designed to retract."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_4",
              "group_title": "A. According to mechanism",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Handheld retractors"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Held by the surgeon or an assistant. Examples include Deaver, Richardson and Morris retractors."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Self-retaining retractors"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maintain separation through a frame or locking mechanism. Examples include Balfour and Bookwalter systems."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_5",
              "group_title": "B. According to purpose",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Category",
                    "Examples",
                    "Main function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Wound-edge retraction",
                      "Richardson, Morris",
                      "Retract the abdominal wall and wound edges"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Deep retraction",
                      "Deaver",
                      "Retract deep tissues and selected viscera"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Flexible retraction",
                      "Malleable ribbon retractor",
                      "Adapt its shape to the operative anatomy"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Broad, sustained retraction",
                      "Balfour, Bookwalter",
                      "Maintain a larger abdominal opening"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Organ protection or displacement",
                      "Broad-bladed retractors, selected malleable retractors",
                      "Gently move or shield organs to improve access"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The classifications overlap: a retractor may be suitable for both wound-edge and deep retraction depending on its size and design."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "abdominal_retractor_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The parts depend on the specific abdominal retractor being examined. A typical handheld retractor may have the following components."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "quizlet.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Handle — provides a secure grip and control.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Shaft or neck — connects the handle to the blade and provides reach.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blade — contacts the tissue and holds it away from the operative field.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blade tip or edge — the terminal part of the blade; should be smooth and undamaged.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Curvature or angle — allows the blade to follow the body contour or reach deeper structures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "A self-retaining abdominal retractor may additionally have:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "A frame or crossbar.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Adjustable arms.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Detachable blades.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A ratchet, screw or locking mechanism.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE tip: Do not label every abdominal retractor as though it has the same design. Identify the specific model first, then label the components actually present."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "abdominal_retractor_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "4. Important abdominal retractors you must distinguish",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Richardson retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Handheld retractor with a broad, generally right-angled blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Commonly used for retracting abdominal wound edges.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Available in different blade sizes for different depths.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Morris retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Handheld retractor with a broad blade, often curved or angled depending on the design.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used for abdominal wound retraction.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Its broad blade helps distribute contact pressure.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Malleable ribbon retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Long, flat, flexible metal blade that can be shaped to the required contour.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Useful for deep retraction and protecting or displacing selected tissues.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Must be shaped carefully to avoid sharp bends or excessive pressure.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Deaver retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Long handle with a broad, deeply curved blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Commonly used for deep abdominal retraction and displacement of selected viscera.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/abdominal_retractor_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Balfour retractor"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Self-retaining frame with two lateral blades and commonly a central blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintains exposure during open abdominal surgery without continuous manual holding.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "abdominal_retractor_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "5. Uses and surgical applications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Abdominal retractors are used to:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Retract the skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle and fascial edges.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain the opening created by an abdominal incision.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Improve visualisation of intra-abdominal structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Displace selected organs to expose deeper operative anatomy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Protect adjacent structures during dissection.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain a stable operative field during suturing, ligation and other surgical steps.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_9",
              "group_title": "Examples of procedures",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Procedure",
                    "Retraction requirement"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Laparotomy",
                      "Separate abdominal wound edges"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Appendicectomy through an open approach",
                      "Improve access to the operative field"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Open cholecystectomy",
                      "Assist exposure of the upper abdomen"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bowel resection",
                      "Maintain wound exposure and facilitate access to bowel"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Colectomy",
                      "Provide sustained abdominal access"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Open hysterectomy",
                      "Maintain exposure of the abdominal incision"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Splenectomy",
                      "Assist exposure of the left upper abdominal field"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The choice depends on the incision, the depth of the operative field, the structures involved and whether continuous manual control or self-retaining exposure is preferred."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "abdominal_retractor_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "6. Principles of safe use",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Select a retractor of appropriate size and shape.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the blade for rough edges, bending, cracks or other damage.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place the blade against the intended tissue rather than blindly pushing into the wound.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply the minimum force needed to achieve adequate exposure.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid trapping bowel, blood vessels, nerves or other vulnerable structures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reassess tissue colour, tension and perfusion during prolonged retraction.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Communicate with the surgeon when the retractor obstructs access or the tissue appears under excessive tension.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Release tension carefully before repositioning or removing the instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "abdominal_retractor_group_11",
              "group_title": "Why is tissue perfusion important?",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sustained compression can reduce blood flow to tissue. This may lead to ischaemia, tissue damage and delayed healing. Deep retraction can also injure organs if the blade is incorrectly positioned or excessive force is applied."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
